articles.content_en:
When the People's Court tries or enforces a case involving a negotiable instrument dispute, it may, upon application by a party and provision of security, take preservation measures or enforcement measures according to law against an instrument in any of the following circumstances:
(1) an instrument held by a party to the instrument who fails to perform agreed obligations and has a direct creditor-debt relationship with the instrument debtor;
(2) an instrument acquired by the holder in bad faith;
(3) an instrument held by a holder who should have paid consideration but failed to pay consideration;
(4) an instrument bearing the phrase "non-negotiable" and used for discounting;
(5) an instrument bearing the phrase "non-negotiable" and used for pledge;
(6) an instrument in other circumstances provided by law or judicial interpretation.
III. Burden of Adducing Evidence

Where an instrument debtor raises any of the following defenses against a holder in accordance with Article 12 and Article 13 of the Negotiable Instruments Law, the People's Court shall support it:
(1) the holder has a direct creditor-debt relationship with the instrument debtor and fails to perform agreed obligations;
(2) the holder acquired the instrument through illegal means such as fraud, theft, or coercion, or, knowing of the existence of the foregoing circumstances, acquired the instrument in bad faith;
(3) the holder acquired the instrument knowing that there were grounds for defense between the instrument debtor and the drawer or a prior party of the holder;
(4) the holder acquired the instrument through gross negligence;
(5) other circumstances where the holder may not enjoy rights on the instrument according to law.

Where an instrument debtor raises any of the following defenses against a holder in accordance with Article 9, Article 17, Article 18, Article 22, and Article 31 of the Negotiable Instruments Law, the People's Court shall support it:
(1) the instrument lacks statutory necessary particulars or does not conform to the statutory form;
(2) the limitation period for rights on the instrument has expired;
(3) a judgment on the invalidation of the instrument rendered by the People's Court has already become legally effective;
(4) the instrument was acquired by endorsement but the endorsements are not consecutive;
(5) other circumstances where the holder may not enjoy rights on the instrument according to law.

Where the person who lost an instrument applies to the People's Court for a public summons within three days after notifying the payer of the instrument to stop payment due to loss, the application for the public summons shall specify the following:
(1) the amount of the instrument;
(2) the drawer, holder, and endorser;
(3) the reasons and facts of the application;
(4) the time of notifying the payer or agent payer of the instrument to stop payment due to loss;
(5) the name, mailing address, telephone number, etc. of the payer or agent payer.

Where the signature or seal affixed by the drawer on an instrument does not conform to the Negotiable Instruments Law and the following provisions, such signature or seal shall not have effect under the Negotiable Instruments Law:
(1) the signature or seal of the drawer on a commercial bill of exchange shall be the financial seal or official seal of the legal person or the unit plus the signature or seal of its legal representative, the person in charge of the unit, or its authorized agent;
(2) the signature or seal of the drawer on a bank bill of exchange and the signature or seal of the acceptor of a bank acceptance bill shall be the special seal for bank bills of the bank plus the signature or seal of its legal representative or its authorized agent;
(3) the signature or seal of the drawer on a bank promissory note shall be the special seal for promissory notes of the bank plus the signature or seal of its legal representative or its authorized agent;
(4) the signature or seal of the drawer on a check: if the drawer is a unit, it shall be the financial seal or official seal consistent with the seal reserved by the unit at the bank plus the signature or seal of its legal representative or its authorized agent; if the drawer is an individual, it shall be the signature or seal consistent with the seal reserved by the individual at the bank.

Article 65: Where an instrument has any of the following circumstances and has not been transferred by endorsement, the instrument debtor shall not bear instrument liability; where it has been transferred by endorsement, the invalidity of the instrument shall not affect the validity of other genuine signatures and seals:
(1) the drawer's signature or seal is not genuine;
(2) the drawer is a person without capacity for civil conduct;
(3) the drawer is a person with limited capacity for civil conduct.

Article 69: Where a payer or its agent payer has any of the following circumstances, it shall bear liability on its own:
(1) making erroneous payment by failing to perform the obligation of examination, in accordance with the provisions of Article 57 of the Law on Negotiable Instruments, of the legal identity certificate or valid documents of the person presenting the instrument and of the continuity of endorsements on the bill of exchange;
(2) paying an instrument subject to public summons for exigence during the period of public summons for exigence;
(3) making payment after receiving a notice of cessation of payment from the People's Court;
(4) making payment in other circumstances with malice or gross negligence.

Article 70: The "other relevant certificates" referred to in Article 63 of the Law on Negotiable Instruments mean:
(1) certificates or legal instruments issued by the People's Court declaring the acceptor or payer missing or deceased;
(2) certificates issued by public security organs proving that the acceptor or payer has absconded or whose whereabouts are unknown;
(3) certificates issued by hospitals or relevant units proving the death of the acceptor or payer;
(4) documents issued by notarization organs having the effect of a certificate of refusal.
An announcement made and published by the acceptor itself indicating that it has no ability to pay the amount of the instrument may be recognized as a certificate of refusal.
